, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37600/selection/ma-v1/a6947ffc0aa2e82631d22f6d5978111d1ec16d93f02fec2bb19dbb879497c7ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection/c57d9d0b8b9c3226799efd32e5e134e5b1e4a15d3156e7dff7cf7f748e16e6d4" :answer/id #uuid "7171b1e3-4ad3-5a0e-8508-b06e94f26fa6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection/044ca34ddcdb1fd26a51d1506994fa2e6d37c41f8c932b4c4757e1447651099c" :answer/id #uuid "88f7a7ec-2f2b-5ecc-9670-495e622df40a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection/07f80a62f57633d23a6fc4cf9e9ba39241b2b353a24f9fe382f4ce5921190834" :answer/id #uuid "1537fcef-344f-5edf-a4c8-1a05735a7626" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection/46a984604c4bbde0e723e9ca8f487cde4e8924b40a3e9d932cf1039767812b3e" :answer/id #uuid "ee6f6b31-e4f8-5d97-b461-445a2a2e4298" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection/50e38a17627c4ef4aa05cd75de991e7ccd40b7f733ea70d6a8d12179b7852f2a" :answer/id #uuid "ae411d36-4325-5ced-aa15-4b752f5a204f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4bcdb19-e5dd-5171-975b-98cf4a9a14ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80392/selection/044ca34ddcdb1fd26a51d1506994fa2e6d37c41f8c932b4c4757e1447651099c" :answer-field/choices ["q-80392/selection/c57d9d0b8b9c3226799efd32e5e134e5b1e4a15d3156e7dff7cf7f748e16e6d4" "q-80392/selection/044ca34ddcdb1fd26a51d1506994fa2e6d37c41f8c932b4c4757e1447651099c" "q-80392/selection/07f80a62f57633d23a6fc4cf9e9ba39241b2b353a24f9fe382f4ce5921190834" "q-80392/selection/46a984604c4bbde0e723e9ca8f487cde4e8924b40a3e9d932cf1039767812b3e" "q-80392/selection/50e38a17627c4ef4aa05cd75de991e7ccd40b7f733ea70d6a8d12179b7852f2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80392" :question/id #uuid "84388249-87e9-543c-a81f-fd99783cee24" :question/math-academy-id "q-80392" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $B$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & -3 \\\\ c & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=5,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80392/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80392"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection/7322b6b91a33ad05ae674e76827f9ee18eae64c9cc55b7e0854e9aad6ffe59fa" :answer/id #uuid "c4a102b2-e01d-5251-8ef7-b201142b182b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection/757e73be9fa212e992556ca395a4a6a7891a770e8210186fb178d2d91a2d7ad3" :answer/id #uuid "b557fb4c-9fd5-534e-bf1c-8ca53dbccba5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection/356feaefa221578556d6824876d56aa471548726ecf1b9797be54e612e582e44" :answer/id #uuid "f4e9417e-df3e-51ac-abac-071a5d55aea4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection/c498b09c08a14fa256c60342c919e14180d5b7947fe0a548a6799f6dacc1b299" :answer/id #uuid "f898f5e8-8348-5564-ae06-ea466aeb8422" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection/07f80a62f57633d23a6fc4cf9e9ba39241b2b353a24f9fe382f4ce5921190834" :answer/id #uuid "fc03c7f3-b7d2-5764-ba8b-a11b7b5cba7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec3a3559-e641-59ec-96ed-ca209bf685c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80371/selection/757e73be9fa212e992556ca395a4a6a7891a770e8210186fb178d2d91a2d7ad3" :answer-field/choices ["q-80371/selection/7322b6b91a33ad05ae674e76827f9ee18eae64c9cc55b7e0854e9aad6ffe59fa" "q-80371/selection/757e73be9fa212e992556ca395a4a6a7891a770e8210186fb178d2d91a2d7ad3" "q-80371/selection/356feaefa221578556d6824876d56aa471548726ecf1b9797be54e612e582e44" "q-80371/selection/c498b09c08a14fa256c60342c919e14180d5b7947fe0a548a6799f6dacc1b299" "q-80371/selection/07f80a62f57633d23a6fc4cf9e9ba39241b2b353a24f9fe382f4ce5921190834"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80371" :question/id #uuid "1022364c-f82a-59dc-a49c-9a4352aa75a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-80371" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $K$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80371/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80371"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection/f672e45fbea4aa04a2636fa70f16790e00cdb7c16d9976c51e8a2004a64cce58" :answer/id #uuid "ee9dd13c-21b2-53d9-bd93-6975ce578bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "8834a7c9-ee24-5c0d-9840-34fb22108046" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection/193f68462ae7d909e0ce212199410ac261d103ac1f3a6ac738f1d0102d00be7b" :answer/id #uuid "58cceb92-58fe-5156-989f-0b2a057fc8bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection/34a1aa247866ea4125372676a87fe9f11332062a5162191e3ecc2c81abaee976" :answer/id #uuid "62b3a655-cb21-5490-b639-285b81db3c5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection/7cb397bfb66f537e5500485cd321efe72a11b2daf515b29904e51f831d8ef283" :answer/id #uuid "a848b508-0c63-53ba-aa21-33ee30a038f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1b537c-5521-5da0-8038-c727415e4ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140041/selection/f672e45fbea4aa04a2636fa70f16790e00cdb7c16d9976c51e8a2004a64cce58" :answer-field/choices ["q-140041/selection/f672e45fbea4aa04a2636fa70f16790e00cdb7c16d9976c51e8a2004a64cce58" "q-140041/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-140041/selection/193f68462ae7d909e0ce212199410ac261d103ac1f3a6ac738f1d0102d00be7b" "q-140041/selection/34a1aa247866ea4125372676a87fe9f11332062a5162191e3ecc2c81abaee976" "q-140041/selection/7cb397bfb66f537e5500485cd321efe72a11b2daf515b29904e51f831d8ef283"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140041" :question/id #uuid "6d92b551-0b27-59ea-861b-c8ea209a5b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140041" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-(1)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140041/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140041"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection/4dbf1e2e2e5575452454e5f9394a96eec608f5c4a6ee078e67c0db49e36fc5fe" :answer/id #uuid "6995fb43-392f-5e19-9886-2fbd7a7d3cc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection/0c95931fd618aeb4252b669f88524364fb9ac8d16c372516552e96e61e6cad42" :answer/id #uuid "797b7564-7732-5ee2-89f4-b4f6cce5a62f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection/116ba511f78f3ed888236c80aa0db20008860383f2b47eb517f411d99b4bfe21" :answer/id #uuid "8e97a551-c9e0-5f2e-a443-570545382b8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "592a6c5c-9978-572d-ab14-5910f7cba97d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection/eed85fff1c310132f0359c50f92366bdd4fa19a46b8ed174cc9094aeae2f725d" :answer/id #uuid "fb0253d4-53f9-564a-934f-70c5c7898ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "60764fa2-29f3-5548-9c8d-a7b1082a4a14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80454/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer-field/choices ["q-80454/selection/4dbf1e2e2e5575452454e5f9394a96eec608f5c4a6ee078e67c0db49e36fc5fe" "q-80454/selection/0c95931fd618aeb4252b669f88524364fb9ac8d16c372516552e96e61e6cad42" "q-80454/selection/116ba511f78f3ed888236c80aa0db20008860383f2b47eb517f411d99b4bfe21" "q-80454/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-80454/selection/eed85fff1c310132f0359c50f92366bdd4fa19a46b8ed174cc9094aeae2f725d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80454" :question/id #uuid "c4757e3d-a1ca-57f2-bdd0-190f98bd42fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80454" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80454/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80454"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection/ced1901190afe1410177425b94d7ac3de27482c59c1a076505febced6e6c4e2b" :answer/id #uuid "c5c6b6f8-11d3-5bd1-992c-ce9a42218c43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "4d3cee8e-b25b-5aa2-b0ff-0f5bc51b7160" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection/0dfb9f96f41fcd67cdfcde5ec13d7faa67d63a0533589bf590490e76ceca53a4" :answer/id #uuid "341322f0-967c-56f7-a906-b4cf00ce1327" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection/c2b4e34afc2c763a79d25da189720d891903dc9d39163bb5c2e6c05f71743655" :answer/id #uuid "c928d6ae-1544-52ad-8b1d-532d7a69a690" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection/e005587e89679afb8093f9dcc42fbf1a76f9da95b653d478cc25538f3353f562" :answer/id #uuid "4a5fbf87-5480-5c84-9a94-80c8dafe4f73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "893177bf-d746-52fd-ade7-32faaeed53c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80453/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer-field/choices ["q-80453/selection/ced1901190afe1410177425b94d7ac3de27482c59c1a076505febced6e6c4e2b" "q-80453/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-80453/selection/0dfb9f96f41fcd67cdfcde5ec13d7faa67d63a0533589bf590490e76ceca53a4" "q-80453/selection/c2b4e34afc2c763a79d25da189720d891903dc9d39163bb5c2e6c05f71743655" "q-80453/selection/e005587e89679afb8093f9dcc42fbf1a76f9da95b653d478cc25538f3353f562"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80453" :question/id #uuid "00550df2-9915-5f38-9065-539bd0966fe6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80453" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-(-2)I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80453/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80453"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection/725440b50e6392bde9ea8c558b0b7c66120d8c8e4d7cf41259813b7cb7baae1a" :answer/id #uuid "17065d17-4eb7-5c27-86fb-33c626b98e07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection/bcd74274e20b2ec3d8aec8efb4af74ef43b3ad2156201bfae5fb43293b442647" :answer/id #uuid "cd96262c-5415-5073-9f57-f40b01533bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "246b7dcc-a1d6-532c-b83d-012038bef7de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection/1e7927896a40c2a07b1833b359c9f89aee921f837efb88145469f6603b094434" :answer/id #uuid "1e4cc75a-c249-5c14-b6c2-046927dabc5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection/4a27f1e765d631c25c52fc52dd58408f7961344fdbec7eacb068e12ccfad10c3" :answer/id #uuid "b8ec39a1-2872-544a-8145-aabe3cc392dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "845b12d1-c113-5f21-97cc-64bc021cfebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37629/selection/1e7927896a40c2a07b1833b359c9f89aee921f837efb88145469f6603b094434" :answer-field/choices ["q-37629/selection/725440b50e6392bde9ea8c558b0b7c66120d8c8e4d7cf41259813b7cb7baae1a" "q-37629/selection/bcd74274e20b2ec3d8aec8efb4af74ef43b3ad2156201bfae5fb43293b442647" "q-37629/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37629/selection/1e7927896a40c2a07b1833b359c9f89aee921f837efb88145469f6603b094434" "q-37629/selection/4a27f1e765d631c25c52fc52dd58408f7961344fdbec7eacb068e12ccfad10c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37629" :question/id #uuid "a70cd34e-7f71-566e-9b48-b1d57ca344ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37629" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37629/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37629"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125004 :question/answer-fields 17592186123046] {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/752419a5e0333ef9c9d916e1b9bea9dc7a8e79bc6fb8af20d26d9d10d8d92a4b" :answer/id #uuid "5a4ab1ac-b69c-5165-b41d-693f838ef8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/bc3e999677d2c45bb5bbc2f432b6caac9f593c7faef7d0306d4c53b3b31d95fb" :answer/id #uuid "e18643b7-9ad1-53e1-a2ae-17283258eb07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/377d333a57989e5591c94f8beb314de8a39ac57a7b8f407c44d1884e7c3b755d" :answer/id #uuid "50da3090-6e20-50de-939c-75fb166647f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "81869670-0c66-58ba-a79f-d87d79303f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/20872b10bb86d27c36d100579ff8e209ce53ad2a6fd2d3e10501b5b79b1b9a54" :answer/id #uuid "20196e23-63f2-5208-b1ea-0d80f2c19665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c" :answer-field/id #uuid "aeee0811-d75d-5d71-8afe-7ee4a5918974" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer-field/choices ["q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/752419a5e0333ef9c9d916e1b9bea9dc7a8e79bc6fb8af20d26d9d10d8d92a4b" "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/bc3e999677d2c45bb5bbc2f432b6caac9f593c7faef7d0306d4c53b3b31d95fb" "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/377d333a57989e5591c94f8beb314de8a39ac57a7b8f407c44d1884e7c3b755d" "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c/20872b10bb86d27c36d100579ff8e209ce53ad2a6fd2d3e10501b5b79b1b9a54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37609"] :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-1) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-3I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37609/selection/ma-v1/be3bb9a38649f29d2e6c8706411ae0f5c484c14c94c1ca651e32cd6d111ed62c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" :answer/id #uuid "ab8c9289-2ca2-5c89-b71f-6b7e99400776" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection/a64189931ea8bea8b327ff02de88f18f3275caec4f7a980dc17103f5c1bc31fb" :answer/id #uuid "7ba54832-6e12-59f2-bd09-fc5a1b97086a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "77515f09-1e70-561c-90b5-01b19faecde6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection/8b8a36d96bbf76ba1018298ac259106e51224db0adb10c038acbf29fba382784" :answer/id #uuid "a19ea69b-2ab2-50a7-b54a-63418a610d53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection/c0bb241511534a82ed83bf9f68ac61e0b32368db71a28bd7cf3b67bde4af7283" :answer/id #uuid "19e9db10-5948-5eea-a3c8-02ee13f4a8ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "10b79f7a-80a5-5aa9-9a58-f7598e6dd1eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37105/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" :answer-field/choices ["q-37105/selection/89509341da3fc059d3fae3c08ea87f7c7273dd2cfed4f405dd9f061ed448811c" "q-37105/selection/a64189931ea8bea8b327ff02de88f18f3275caec4f7a980dc17103f5c1bc31fb" "q-37105/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" "q-37105/selection/8b8a36d96bbf76ba1018298ac259106e51224db0adb10c038acbf29fba382784" "q-37105/selection/c0bb241511534a82ed83bf9f68ac61e0b32368db71a28bd7cf3b67bde4af7283"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37105" :question/id #uuid "95218e31-b9a4-5377-9415-d077552ae76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-37105" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-(1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37105/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37105"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection/ad362d7ed109b6a7fddafec2294f88d02e0a983775d131c6b2ec5ca62befc3b7" :answer/id #uuid "6e32ce35-1563-5bd9-8946-434c332f076a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection/316e31382540e65d0f46665a667fc04a682c6856ba5ccfe153e95664a68b2948" :answer/id #uuid "b30ec5a3-856c-5987-85c9-4fcddffa926e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection/0ea84eddbb86c801755798ff5359850de462b3ab59bf3f8027cfb439e37a1853" :answer/id #uuid "814714a3-6ec6-54e2-9d83-6b5ab94883f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection/83e1afda14b7828dd32335bb4b02d9e461e6f35eda76b7346cb6b7a1789f445c" :answer/id #uuid "400c897a-e7be-52b5-91cb-2ed277aa1e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c" :answer/id #uuid "6bbb9458-fb18-5571-8136-c01aebced69a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65c1e864-5cd7-5eab-8a53-0af240d1b84d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80458/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-80458/selection/ad362d7ed109b6a7fddafec2294f88d02e0a983775d131c6b2ec5ca62befc3b7" "q-80458/selection/316e31382540e65d0f46665a667fc04a682c6856ba5ccfe153e95664a68b2948" "q-80458/selection/0ea84eddbb86c801755798ff5359850de462b3ab59bf3f8027cfb439e37a1853" "q-80458/selection/83e1afda14b7828dd32335bb4b02d9e461e6f35eda76b7346cb6b7a1789f445c" "q-80458/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80458" :question/id #uuid "21d5e4ed-55a5-585d-b95d-dc1afd92b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80458" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-80458/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "ca525aa1-7ed0-5fdd-9d96-0e9b2edd1494" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection/7cf029c3ab18e89829906bc75ea8bccab0149c53f5ce25583e1200fd91e8d36e" :answer/id #uuid "76ffa596-d73d-5bd6-9263-65390ba60c37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection/a98b3b253bfcf7abf8ec65fc3343a0885fc4195d7023a1cd8dafa18976576c14" :answer/id #uuid "4b444acc-c4ff-5eb6-b3bb-01c565a69ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection/fc4e2cf106841016abafd2875d67ff73af65a59d2ae61b8b12fe8b80bbbe07ac" :answer/id #uuid "d798c578-da9c-52b0-a900-814e43037f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection/ed3e5772ada91df6851b168e6a1c19726b0e2e4ebaec70bec9c0dc7fa837322a" :answer/id #uuid "f033d876-c891-59a5-b1e7-9a892a46d589" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fdd7678-53f2-5486-9127-5d8f562a93a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37108/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer-field/choices ["q-37108/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-37108/selection/7cf029c3ab18e89829906bc75ea8bccab0149c53f5ce25583e1200fd91e8d36e" "q-37108/selection/a98b3b253bfcf7abf8ec65fc3343a0885fc4195d7023a1cd8dafa18976576c14" "q-37108/selection/fc4e2cf106841016abafd2875d67ff73af65a59d2ae61b8b12fe8b80bbbe07ac" "q-37108/selection/ed3e5772ada91df6851b168e6a1c19726b0e2e4ebaec70bec9c0dc7fa837322a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37108" :question/id #uuid "7657fa03-feb0-5456-ac01-2b7532a0349e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37108" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37108/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37108"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "9b22ec08-4ec6-5651-b401-3ff9373f675b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "fb79e3f1-2467-5bc6-b857-7abb124b0297" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "5f3efb99-ff7b-555e-9906-98ef65c32255" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "0f7511a5-11a5-5d8f-a897-936071fc2212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "313e4e9c-5ff8-5643-9d14-94404a790626" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0670e83-dd94-56fe-b82d-b9851e10784f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37107/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-37107/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-37107/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-37107/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-37107/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-37107/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37107" :question/id #uuid "5dcdcbab-de97-5139-9022-3e17c774b429" :question/math-academy-id "q-37107" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & -1 \\\\ b & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of  $\\frac{b}{a}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nSo, the  first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -7\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37107/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37107"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "47f39b33-d572-5669-9535-608b12445cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "13ce2cb4-7bf4-51ba-9d46-bbb5e4e6bd43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "30064e04-ebc9-5f83-9a12-630051f13751" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "8f66646b-302d-5101-9bdf-67dfc037a700" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c" :answer/id #uuid "16b25b5e-9024-54a2-8a5d-bc6d9b719401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "de003c30-a4a3-591d-b499-8f0d312676e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80457/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-80457/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-80457/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-80457/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-80457/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-80457/selection/9e11fa4df3725c69efaf2fb0a82d90e47a4519d002f721e23606c3d6290fb02c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80457" :question/id #uuid "3097be51-a6e3-5d6b-b1df-263db16e20b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80457" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-80457/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80457"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection/93ac20e1d2d366a305d790b7cde6e9c82416c8210e4fe203a7b95363ba90cd1c" :answer/id #uuid "747a8e90-e095-5cc3-a755-b85f9e2fcc7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection/02452005c9768dfbfcfb9ef87df33af11ea79ce99da2b32b1935e6fefee26657" :answer/id #uuid "f3e14f11-f84e-5f57-96c5-06e515c32839" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection/9de99120ba3e4cd0fdf9b598e869d813353d8d398966b706c4300324a682ac74" :answer/id #uuid "5ca49fda-4097-520b-b0c1-647643f781a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection/2e03b0d8ab38f04c76df48bfe01b71eeb5bd493ce502d9742b51c2ce1533b53e" :answer/id #uuid "84224f5c-919c-5e7a-8b16-344c3e38ceca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "feee2e02-90d8-5f92-8aff-ebfcb1ec5ea5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a432a26f-8b9a-579c-9296-ee43131ba45d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37608/selection/93ac20e1d2d366a305d790b7cde6e9c82416c8210e4fe203a7b95363ba90cd1c" :answer-field/choices ["q-37608/selection/93ac20e1d2d366a305d790b7cde6e9c82416c8210e4fe203a7b95363ba90cd1c" "q-37608/selection/02452005c9768dfbfcfb9ef87df33af11ea79ce99da2b32b1935e6fefee26657" "q-37608/selection/9de99120ba3e4cd0fdf9b598e869d813353d8d398966b706c4300324a682ac74" "q-37608/selection/2e03b0d8ab38f04c76df48bfe01b71eeb5bd493ce502d9742b51c2ce1533b53e" "q-37608/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37608" :question/id #uuid "363f53f1-8928-520d-b1b8-50ceeff39d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37608" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:  \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37608/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37608"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection/97d7485a9bfdcf93bb0dd4fdd387979176cc451e9b1a3cdb3e642af89543a21b" :answer/id #uuid "2adb872e-3143-54f8-b136-44da5ca2a356" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$  and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection/48ebbdc9809f7db521b278c0fd14937bfb7024f97bbcab1451a89717374ca921" :answer/id #uuid "8d2710c3-c22b-56b1-80c6-0a82af8eccf9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection/4e89fef022c66e8791ad8a91f77132b4a3693400c6c7688e4b516485c98df597" :answer/id #uuid "d93f0bf5-0fc2-5a6a-bb1b-d1a3f9242032" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "7b34140c-a50b-50c2-a8b8-5313884cac69" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection/8f36d211ba516e1541b14f795d57bbee38407cf4718a20efdfe45422d50f6db5" :answer/id #uuid "43389d83-a750-5f04-ba00-eb2dd88efff1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff8374a-e0e2-53e8-b86f-a158fe3ef609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37088/selection/97d7485a9bfdcf93bb0dd4fdd387979176cc451e9b1a3cdb3e642af89543a21b" :answer-field/choices ["q-37088/selection/97d7485a9bfdcf93bb0dd4fdd387979176cc451e9b1a3cdb3e642af89543a21b" "q-37088/selection/48ebbdc9809f7db521b278c0fd14937bfb7024f97bbcab1451a89717374ca921" "q-37088/selection/4e89fef022c66e8791ad8a91f77132b4a3693400c6c7688e4b516485c98df597" "q-37088/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37088/selection/8f36d211ba516e1541b14f795d57bbee38407cf4718a20efdfe45422d50f6db5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37088" :question/id #uuid "73d12e91-b996-5b04-bc26-e6f1ba8817f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-37088" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ using its characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-6-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(8)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -24 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 8\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37088/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37088"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection/06613f84a02184dbf1e6c9ca47f3400922859afee2dc806a1488d704d782fef3" :answer/id #uuid "df3b377a-a40e-57d7-b5c3-84e8a02ead1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection/34b3543809622f30814b50cf09b6148b5abf529f36fc8cf29d266361ec315343" :answer/id #uuid "ef2916a3-8c5e-5587-8081-ea117ce7f96e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection/182c3c985df90759b03c135d4c7e75de497168e4a6020577933d3ffa337afef8" :answer/id #uuid "1e4fb40e-ec48-5a93-8973-aaaa476062e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$E$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection/1802229919368a1e127fe2233edb9eb68ea11ee76aee6524510b426c56cb4638" :answer/id #uuid "a0eb97ad-9f11-596b-a0e2-a5b7a107b778" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection/080eb65d0ed3f587c06e23100d1bca00e0cff4fbe89b030e3b2abc0d30c70fe8" :answer/id #uuid "75e03b8f-9278-55d9-9ba4-686c4ed2d076" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e757ab1c-c063-53b5-9971-b463323e964d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-80480/selection/080eb65d0ed3f587c06e23100d1bca00e0cff4fbe89b030e3b2abc0d30c70fe8" :answer-field/choices ["q-80480/selection/06613f84a02184dbf1e6c9ca47f3400922859afee2dc806a1488d704d782fef3" "q-80480/selection/34b3543809622f30814b50cf09b6148b5abf529f36fc8cf29d266361ec315343" "q-80480/selection/182c3c985df90759b03c135d4c7e75de497168e4a6020577933d3ffa337afef8" "q-80480/selection/1802229919368a1e127fe2233edb9eb68ea11ee76aee6524510b426c56cb4638" "q-80480/selection/080eb65d0ed3f587c06e23100d1bca00e0cff4fbe89b030e3b2abc0d30c70fe8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-80480" :question/id #uuid "ed2d92a1-06e0-595c-a958-5ce3d63de1d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-80480" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80480/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-80480"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection/9c0d4a7c33909c4a82356336acd0712eb9b2a5421a3ebd4239bd76dfcd2e1402" :answer/id #uuid "a6d92175-fa2e-5cf3-83f5-e82cf225cc2b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "0f306b0d-86a2-5b31-b6fc-98874112414e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection/2ff8e33695560ea753b2ea4854472f7b45903078792b92738779c0d84bf40343" :answer/id #uuid "cab45579-9d99-5602-a5de-7ab45da25216" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection/3343efa2de3a210bf3ea81712afc8c56dfb4be7f8022eb3d48ab90442dfe1ba5" :answer/id #uuid "16924c86-72f6-53f8-8e7c-719d02b7ca89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection/b65660ee5bdba422f32a0f3bbeafe2a6ea4f70771fa1e4ddcc486d1ff22abb33" :answer/id #uuid "e781750e-f339-5376-aa76-feca24822b28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "20837c8e-968e-53ae-9a41-e4fb54728aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36914/selection/b65660ee5bdba422f32a0f3bbeafe2a6ea4f70771fa1e4ddcc486d1ff22abb33" :answer-field/choices ["q-36914/selection/9c0d4a7c33909c4a82356336acd0712eb9b2a5421a3ebd4239bd76dfcd2e1402" "q-36914/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-36914/selection/2ff8e33695560ea753b2ea4854472f7b45903078792b92738779c0d84bf40343" "q-36914/selection/3343efa2de3a210bf3ea81712afc8c56dfb4be7f8022eb3d48ab90442dfe1ba5" "q-36914/selection/b65660ee5bdba422f32a0f3bbeafe2a6ea4f70771fa1e4ddcc486d1ff22abb33"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36914" :question/id #uuid "452b1c45-f344-59d0-8f2d-8526fb787055" :question/math-academy-id "q-36914" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36914/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36914"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection/585af983186a8d93381b2f64a1a534f863313ead4bb9193b815da9852af8bde8" :answer/id #uuid "40b54c04-fd69-5a26-ab3f-9500095b483d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "7917d3fe-72ff-5aaf-8d0a-8364bfed0a81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection/3e93a7ca4924699206fc867ff71f0ac375dab668e6a726842bdb9b9cbc5c3947" :answer/id #uuid "fb766637-1864-5ffe-9c7e-2a25126f2065" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection/df25ded00bfe6c56ed829459d9fee513e2dae7c940b63205d853dd9e4a98cd4a" :answer/id #uuid "a9c3bb3a-be59-5524-b6e9-d5f530c3b052" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection/2e5daaf988f8fcc6f9ff9fa322c96e12c0e90b93eb7f222b6f1799f6a560ef4a" :answer/id #uuid "27c01e01-7ee8-52e8-8d9b-68aed4d3b740" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a191dc-0130-5fb7-9b64-41f161a6b503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140055/selection/3e93a7ca4924699206fc867ff71f0ac375dab668e6a726842bdb9b9cbc5c3947" :answer-field/choices ["q-140055/selection/585af983186a8d93381b2f64a1a534f863313ead4bb9193b815da9852af8bde8" "q-140055/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-140055/selection/3e93a7ca4924699206fc867ff71f0ac375dab668e6a726842bdb9b9cbc5c3947" "q-140055/selection/df25ded00bfe6c56ed829459d9fee513e2dae7c940b63205d853dd9e4a98cd4a" "q-140055/selection/2e5daaf988f8fcc6f9ff9fa322c96e12c0e90b93eb7f222b6f1799f6a560ef4a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140055" :question/id #uuid "8ec40444-64f8-58a7-b09c-d9c17582ccae" :question/math-academy-id "q-140055" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140055/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140055"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8575"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8572"] :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8573"] :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8574"] :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
